{
  "_comment": [
    "Fifty-five spoken closet transcripts, annotated with what a PERSON would say",
    "the answer is — not with what `closet-parse.ts` returns today.",
    "",
    "That distinction is the whole value of the file. Annotating from the parser's",
    "own output would produce a corpus that scores 100% forever and detects",
    "nothing; annotating from human judgement produces a corpus with a real error",
    "rate, and the error rate is the measurement Workstream 9 asked for.",
    "",
    "Nine cases are deliberately beyond the lexicon and are known to be scored",
    "wrong today. They are listed here so nobody deletes one to raise a number:",
    "  cp-007  'a couple of denim jackets' — denim read as the colour, not the cloth",
    "  cp-022  'a rain shell' — no such noun in the lexicon, garment dropped",
    "  cp-023  'gold buttons' — a trim colour claimed as the garment's colour",
    "  cp-024  'a puffer' — no such noun, garment dropped",
    "  cp-028  a correction spoken as its own clause is not attached to the fix",
    "  cp-037  socks are not in the garment lexicon",
    "  cp-038  neither are long johns",
    "  cp-044  'under' does not split a clause, so two garments read as one",
    "  cp-051  'sage green' resolves to green, because green is the longer term",
    "",
    "`fallback` is the expensive field: true means the transcript would spend an",
    "AI call. Eight of fifty-five are annotated true, all of them phrases that",
    "point at something already said, which is genuinely unresolvable alone.",
    "",
    "No transcript names a real person, place, merchant or account. They are",
    "written for this corpus."
  ],
